    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine the measure of the smallest interior angle, we start by understanding that the sum of the interior angles of a triangle is always \\(180^\\circ\\). Given that the measures of the interior angles are in the ratio \\(5:6:7\\), we can represent the angles as \\(5x\\), \\(6x\\), and \\(7x\\), where \\(x\\) is a common multiplier.\n\nThe sum of these angles can be expressed as:\n\\[\n5x + 6x + 7x = 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\nCombining like terms, we get:\n\\[\n18x = 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\nTo find the value of \\(x\\), we divide both sides of the equation by 18:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{180^\\circ}{18} = 10^\\circ\n\\]\n\nNow that we have the value of \\(x\\), we can determine the measure of each angle by substituting \\(x\\) back into the expressions for the angles:\n\\[\n5x = 5 \\times 10^\\circ = 50^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n6x = 6 \\times 10^\\circ = 60^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n7x = 7 \\times 10^\\circ = 70^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThe smallest angle is \\(50^\\circ\\). Therefore, the measure of the smallest interior angle is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{50}\n\\]",
